The `create_latest_version_view.sql` macro that is in the documentation ignores the alias of the original versioned model.  That might be undesirable, e.g. if you have a custom `generate_alias_name` macro.

Here is an alternative variation that uses a regex to find and strip the specific `_v123` version substring from the alias, without changing anything else in the alias.  This substring must occur at the end of the model name, or must be followed by a delimiter, e.g. it will change `my_model_v123` --> `my_model`, or `my_model_v123_deploy` --> `my_model_deploy`, but not `my_model_v123deploy` which would remain unchanged.

(Just sharing in case it is of interest to anyone else!)

Also, the macro uses `adapter` and an option for unconditionally dropping the view is provided, in case you want to use it with a database engine that does not support `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW`.

<details>
  <summary>
    Click here for the code
  </summary>

```sql
{% macro create_latest_version_view() %}
    {{ return(adapter.dispatch('create_latest_version_view', 'tt_utils')()) }}
{% endmacro %}

{% macro default__create_latest_version_view() %}
    {{ tt_utils._do_create_latest_version_view(can_replace_view=true) }}
{% endmacro %}

-- Implementation copied and adapted from dbt 1.9 documentation:
-- https://docs.getdbt.com/docs/collaborate/govern/model-versions#configuring-database-location-with-alias
--
-- Improvements:
--  * Preserve any other aliasing done with the alias config or generate_alias_name macro by using a regex to strip the
--    version number part.  For example, a model "stg_mydb__customers_v2" that was aliased to "stg_customers_v2_deploy"
--    will result in a view being created as "stg_customers_deploy", rather than "stg_mydb__customers".
--  * Have a way to unconditionally drop the view first for databases that don't support `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW`.
{% macro _do_create_latest_version_view(can_replace_view) %}

    -- this hook will run only if the model is versioned, and only if it's the latest version
    -- otherwise, it's a no-op
    {% if model.get('version') and model.get('version') == model.get('latest_version') %}

        -- this is version substring is copied from what the default generate_alias_name macro generates
        {% set version_search_substring = modules.re.escape("_v" ~ (model.get('version') | replace(".", "_"))) %}
        -- we will look for this version only at the end of the string, or if followed by an underscore, and then
        -- remove only this version substring.
        {% set version_strip_pattern = '(.*)' ~ version_search_substring ~ '((?:_.*)?)' %}
        {% set match = modules.re.fullmatch(version_strip_pattern, this.identifier) %}
        {% if not match %}
            -- We couldn't find the version substring in the identifier.  This is probably due to one of these reasons:
            --  * A generate_alias_name macro was used that did not properly include the version string.
            --  * The user manually aliased the model to a name that does not include the version string.
            --
            -- If this becomes a problem, we may consider whether we want to create this view at all.  A common
            -- scenario might be if the user followed dbt documentation to manually alias latest version of
            -- model 'my_model_v2' to just 'my_model', which would conflict with the view created by this macro.
            -- In this case, we may revisit the decision of throwing an exception, and consider whether we want to
            -- just silently skip creating the view entirely (perhaps logging a message).
            {{ exceptions.raise_compiler_error(
                "Could not find version substring '" ~ version_search_substring ~
                "' in identifier '" ~ this.identifier ~ "'."
            ) }}
        {% endif %}
        {% set new_name = match.group(1, 2) | join %}

        {% set new_relation = this.incorporate(path={"identifier": new_name}) %}

        {% set existing_relation = load_relation(new_relation) %}

        {% if existing_relation and (not existing_relation.is_view or not can_replace_view) %}
            {{ drop_relation_if_exists(existing_relation) }}
        {% endif %}

        {% set create_view_sql -%}
            -- this syntax may vary by data platform
            create {% if can_replace_view -%} or replace {%- endif %} view {{ new_relation }}
                as select * from {{ this }}
        {%- endset %}

        {% do log("Creating view " ~ new_relation ~ " pointing to " ~ this, info = true) if execute %}

        {{ return(create_view_sql) }}

    {% else %}

        -- no-op
        select 1 as id

    {% endif %}

{% endmacro %}
``` 
</details>

[Here is a link to the diff](https://www.diffchecker.com/ZWrXRoW9/) showing what changed from the macro in the dbt documentation.

I had a bit of a headache where the views being created did not receive grants from the grants feature of DBT, since these views aren't associated with the relation directory.

I threw the problem at Gemini to apply the grants and it works great. Would really like more of this behavior in core.

<details>
<summary>Click to expand: Updated create_latest_version_view Macro</summary>

```
{% macro create_latest_version_view() %}

    -- This hook will run only if the model is versioned and it's the latest version.
    -- Otherwise, it's a no-op.
    {% if model.get('version') and model.get('version') == model.get('latest_version') %}

        {# Define the new relation (the view) using the model's base name #}
        {% set new_relation = this.incorporate(path={"identifier": model['name']}) %}

        {# Check if a relation with the same name already exists and if it's not a view.
           If it's a table, drop it to ensure the view can be created without conflicts. #}
        {% set existing_relation = load_relation(new_relation) %}

        {% if existing_relation and not existing_relation.is_view %}
            {% do log("Dropping existing relation " ~ new_relation ~ " which is a table, to create view.", info=true) %}
            {{ drop_relation_if_exists(existing_relation) }}
        {% endif %}

        {# Construct the SQL to create or replace the view, pointing to the latest version of the model #}
        {% set create_view_sql -%}
            create or replace view {{ new_relation }}
              as select * from {{ this }}
        {%- endset %}

        {% do log("Creating view " ~ new_relation ~ " pointing to " ~ this, info = true) if execute %}

        {# Initialize the final SQL string with the view creation statement #}
        {% set final_sql = create_view_sql %}

        {#
            Now, retrieve and apply the grants configured for the model.
            This block accesses `model.config.grants`, which holds grant definitions
            from your `dbt_project.yml` or the model's `config` block.
            The `execute` check ensures this logic runs during compilation.
        #}
        {% if model.config.grants and execute %}
            {% do log("Applying grants for view " ~ new_relation, info=true) %}
            {% for grant_type, grantees in model.config.grants.items() %}
                {% if grantees is iterable and grantees is not string %} {# Ensure grantees is a list of roles/users #}
                    {% for grantee in grantees %}
                        {# Construct the grant SQL statement for each privilege type and grantee #}
                        {# This generic `grant ... on ... to ...` syntax is broadly compatible across SQL dialects. #}
                        {% set grant_sql = "grant " ~ grant_type ~ " on " ~ new_relation ~ " to " ~ grantee ~ ";" %}
                        {% do log("    " ~ grant_sql, info = true) %}
                        {# Append each grant SQL statement to the final SQL, separated by a newline for readability #}
                        {% set final_sql = final_sql ~ "\n" ~ grant_sql %}
                    {% endfor %}
                {% else %}
                    {% do log("Warning: Grants for type '" ~ grant_type ~ "' are not a list and will be skipped for " ~ new_relation ~ ".", info=true) %}
                {% endif %}
            {% endfor %}
        {% else %}
            {% do log("No grants configured or 'execute' is false for view " ~ new_relation, info=true) %}
        {% endif %}

        {# Return the complete SQL string, which dbt will execute #}
        {{ return(final_sql) }}

    {% else %}

        -- If the model is not versioned or not the latest version, perform a no-op.
        select 1 as id

    {% endif %}

{% endmacro %}
```
</details>

