## Alternative (much simpler) idea

_credit to @graciegoheen_

Premises:
- I want the latest version to materialize as the unsuffixed model name
- I don't want to remember to update the `alias` config every time I add a new model version or update `latest_version` config

Solution:
- I can reimplement `generate_alias_name` to check  `node.is_latest_version` and return the unsuffixed model name - or even suffixed with `_latest`. Rather than creating a separate pointer view, this simply aliases the latest version of the model.


Example:
```sql
{% macro generate_alias_name(custom_alias_name=none, node=none) -%}

    {%- if custom_alias_name -%}

        {{ custom_alias_name | trim }}

    {%- elif node.version and not node.is_latest_version -%}

        {{ return(node.name ~ "_v" ~ (node.version | replace(".", "_"))) }}

    {%- else -%}

        {{ node.name }}

    {%- endif -%}

{%- endmacro %}
```

Meaning:
- If my model is versioned and _not_ the latest version, append `_v#`
- If this model is the latest version, just the unsuffixed model name, please

I remember thinking of this a few years ago, and suggesting it to some folks - so there must be some reason why this isn't desirable / sufficient ... ? @Spince @nicohein @alison985 do you remember, or have thoughts one way or the other?

If a custom `generate_alias_name` macro solves for the use case, without any additional complexity (or implementation work) - this could be our recommended pattern, instead of the `create_latest_version_view` post-hook. At minimum I think [we should document it](https://docs.getdbt.com/docs/mesh/govern/model-versions) as a viable path!
